(4774899, 687491, 73235, '<td valign="top">Congrats Oliver. great and very enjoyable picture. Very nicely thought through and arranged.</td>', 'Congrats Oliver. great and very enjoyable picture. Very nicely thought through and arranged.', '2008-06-18 15:11:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(4774939, 687567, 52549, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Debbi:</b></div><hr/><i> How do you get your sensor so clean Irene? Could you share what you do?<br/>Debbi </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Hi Debbi, I use the  <a href="//www.copperhillimages.com/index.php?pr=Tutorials" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Copperhill</a> method. It works fine for me. Although saying that, the first time I used it on my D70 (about 2 years ago) I was so nervous and my hand was shaking so badly, I used 7 swabs before the sensor (it''s actually the filter over the sensor) was really dust free. Nowadays I only need 1 or 2 at the most to get it really clean (and no shaking hands ;-)</td>', 'Originally posted by Debbi: How do you get your sensor so clean Irene? Could you share what you do?Debbi  Hi Debbi, I use the  Copperhill method. It works fine for me. Although saying that, the first time I used it on my D70 (about 2 years ago) I was so nervous and my hand was shaking so badly, I used 7 swabs before the sensor (it''s actually the filter over the sensor) was really dust free. Nowadays I only need 1 or 2 at the most to get it really clean (and no shaking hands ;-)', '2008-06-18 15:35:01.000000', NULL, 0),
